<%#
  The project's FOLDER, shown as the Files panel — the folder's own live files grouped by role
  from their filename (Maker::FileAttachment.role_for), then a collapsed Backups section, then one
  entry per repository found inside it, then every attachment that points OUTSIDE the folder
  ("Linked from elsewhere"). Maker::ProjectFolderListing (@listing) computes the first three;
  ProjectsController#show computes the fourth by diffing @attachments against @listing's own node
  ids.

  THE FOLDER AND THE RELATION ARE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS, AND THE PANEL SHOWS BOTH. `@attachments` is
  the file→project RELATION ProjectsController#show has always computed (detach, cover, addon
  origin, the missing count) — but a file dropped straight into the project's folder on the NAS is
  listed here even though nothing ever attached it: the folder IS the relation for a plain folder
  row, whose `attachment` is nil. A file that IS attached but still lives somewhere else in the
  library renders once, in Linked, with Detach and a button to move it in. `@page_nodes` (PageNodes)
  is what the Linked rows below (and the build-log photos elsewhere on this page) read to find
  their node; a folder row needs no such lookup — its node comes straight off `@listing.files`.

  `@missing_attachment_count` — attached rows whose node ProjectsController#show could not find
  live at all — is unrelated to either list above and is reported on its own, below.

  `@page_thumbs` (ThumbnailMap) is best-effort and INDEPENDENT OF LIVENESS, which is the distinction
  to keep: a backend blip degrades a tile to its format badge, a different condition from a file
  being gone. It is exactly why liveness reads `nodes.present` and not the port, for the folder
  listing as much as for the Linked rows — ThumbnailMap rescues FetchError to {}, so an unmounted
  NAS would otherwise empty this panel and report every file deleted. Pinned by page_nodes_test.rb.

  ONE `thumbs` controller wraps every group rather than one per grid: Stimulus finds targets
  anywhere in the controller element's subtree, so a single instance polls all pending cells on the
  page instead of N instances polling their own slice. `filemenu` rides along on the same element
  for the same reason — one document-level listener, every tile.

  NATIVE <details>, NOT A STIMULUS CONTROLLER: collapsing is what the element is for, it works
  before any JavaScript loads, and `models/_file_tree.html.erb` already uses it for exactly this.
  Open by default, because the panel's job is to show a project's files — Backups is the one
  section that starts closed (see below).
%>
<section class="panel attachments-panel">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <h2>Files</h2>
    <%# The folder's own path, so the panel says WHERE it is showing, not just what. Nothing to
        link to when the folder ensure failed this render (an unmounted NAS, a bucket blip) — the
        upload control below already degrades the same way for the same reason. %>
    <%= link_to @listing.path, files_path(folder: @listing.folder_id), class: "panel-path mono muted" if @listing.available? %>
    <div class="panel-actions">
      <%# "Look at my library NOW", instead of waiting on library_scan's 15 minutes (share/s3) or
          remote_changelog_pull's 2 (chunk_remote). It scans the LIBRARY, not this folder — a scan
          cannot be aimed at a subfolder (Library::Reconciler compares against the whole catalog), so
          the door refreshes the store and the existing chains do the per-project filing. %>
      <%= button_to "Scan folder", project_scan_path(@project), method: :post, class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= link_to "Attach existing", new_project_attachment_path(@project), class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= render "upload_control", upload_url: project_uploads_path(@project),
                 attach_url: project_attachments_path(@project), redirect_url: project_path(@project),
                 parent_node_id: @upload_parent_node_id %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# A share/s3 scan is a BACKGROUND job — an unbounded NAS walk or bucket listing — so the page
      that started it cannot show the result. One reload, to the param-free url, which is what
      stops it looping. Rendered only on the async path: chunk_remote finished inside the request.
      §14d — a data-controller name that resolves to nothing is a SILENT no-op, which is why
      app/javascript/controllers/scanreload_controller.js must exist and stimulus_hygiene_test
      guards that it does. %>
  <% if @scan_started && capabilities.scannable? %>
    <span data-controller="scanreload"
          data-scanreload-url-value="<%= project_path(@project) %>"></span>
  <% end %>

  <%# BACKUPS COME OUT FIRST, and they are keyed on the NODE's name rather than any attachment's
      label — a folder row carries no attachment at all, and Row#backup? always reads the node.
      They keep their own section at the bottom instead of being dropped: a rescue copy is still a
      real file in the project's folder, and a panel that pretended otherwise would be lying about
      what is on disk. %>
  <% backups, live = @listing.files.partition(&:backup?) %>
  <%# Fold anything off-vocabulary into "other" rather than dropping it. Row#role (FileAttachment
      .role_for) is TOTAL over ROLE_LABELS' own keys, so the "other" fallback below cannot trigger
      today — but the render iterates ROLE_LABELS, so without it an unrecognized role would render
      NOWHERE, and a file silently missing from a user's project is a far worse failure than one
      filed under the wrong heading. %>
  <% grouped = live.group_by { |r| Maker::FileAttachment::ROLE_LABELS.key?(r.role) ? r.role : "other" } %>
  <% if live.empty? && backups.empty? && @linked_rows.empty? && @listing.repos.empty? && @missing_attachment_count.zero? && !@listing.truncated? %>
    <p class="muted">No files in this project yet.<% if @listing.available? %> Upload one, or drop files into <span class="mono"><%= @listing.path %></span>.<% end %></p>
  <% else %>
    <div data-controller="thumbs filemenu" data-thumbs-url-value="<%= thumbnails_files_path %>">
      <% Maker::FileAttachment::ROLE_LABELS.each do |role, label| %>
        <% group = grouped[role] %>
        <% next if group.blank? %>
        <%= render "attachment_group", key: role, label: label, rows: group, open: true %>
      <% end %>

      <%# COLLAPSED, and that is the whole of "don't show the backups": they are one click away
          rather than gone, so nothing about the project's folder is hidden from someone looking
          for it. %>
      <% if backups.any? %>
        <%= render "attachment_group", key: "backups", label: "Backups", rows: backups, open: false %>
      <% end %>

      <% if @listing.repos.any? %>
        <%= render "repo_group", repos: @listing.repos %>
      <% end %>

      <%# ATTACHED, BUT LIVING ELSEWHERE. Not a folder row and not counted in @listing.total — its
          own section, always open, so it reads as "these still need your attention" rather than
          being buried among the folder's own files. %>
      <% if @linked_rows.any? %>
        <%= render "attachment_group", key: "linked", label: "Linked from elsewhere", rows: @linked_rows, open: true %>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if @missing_attachment_count.positive? %>
      <p class="muted attachments-missing">
        <%= pluralize(@missing_attachment_count, "attached file") %> no longer in your library.
        <%= link_to "Check the trash", trash_index_path %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# OUTSIDE the empty check on purpose: a walk that hit DIR_CAP before reaching a single file
      would otherwise say "No files in this project yet" and nothing else (found in review). Two
      sentences for two caps — `total` counts only the dirs that were walked, so "0 of 0" is not
      what a cut-short walk means. %>
  <% if @listing.truncated? %>
    <p class="muted attachments-truncated">
      <% if @listing.files.size < @listing.total %>Showing <%= @listing.files.size %> of <%= @listing.total %> files.<% end %>
      <% if @listing.dir_cap_hit? %>Not every subfolder was listed.<% end %>
      <%= link_to "Open the folder", files_path(folder: @listing.folder_id) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</section>
